        "text": "After about five minutes in a warm bath, the tips of your fingers begin to pucker into wrinkles. For years, people assumed this was simply the skin swelling with water, like a sponge. In fact, the wrinkles are produced by your own nervous system. The blood vessels beneath the skin of each fingertip narrow, which pulls the padded skin inward and creates ridges. Because the change is controlled by nerves, a finger with certain nerve damage will not wrinkle at all. Researchers have found that wrinkled fingers grip wet, slippery objects better than smooth ones, which suggests the wrinkles work much like the treads on a tire."
